---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '~/components/common/JsonLd.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';

const metadata = {
  title: `Military-Grade PVC Specifications — SUP Material Standards | ${SITE.name}`,
  description:
    'Technical specifications for military-grade PVC used in inflatable SUPs: denier rating, fabric density, coating weight, tensile strength, and how to evaluate PVC quality when sourcing.',
  ignoreTitleTemplate: true,
};
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Military-Grade PVC Specifications',
  description: metadata.description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name },
  datePublished: '2026-07-27',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <JsonLd data={jsonLd} />
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-sky-600">{SITE.name}</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><a
        href="/research"
        class="hover:text-sky-600">Research</a
      ><span class="mx-2">/</span><span class="text-gray-900">Military-Grade PVC</span>
    </nav>
    <div class="mb-4">
      <span
        class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-600 bg-sky-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full"
        >Materials</span
      ><span class="ml-3 text-sm text-gray-400">8 min read</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">Military-Grade PVC Specifications</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 mb-12 leading-relaxed">
      "Military-grade PVC" is a widely used but loosely defined term. Understanding the actual material specifications
      helps you evaluate SUP quality objectively.
    </p>
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-p:text-gray-600">
      <h2>What Military-Grade Actually Means</h2>
      <p>
        In the inflatable watercraft industry, "military-grade" typically refers to PVC fabric meeting or exceeding
        standards originally developed for military inflatable boats (MIL-SPEC). Key specifications include: minimum
        tensile strength of 600 N/cm in the warp direction, minimum tear strength of 100 N, and UV resistance per
        MIL-STD-810. However, the term is not legally regulated — any manufacturer can claim "military-grade" regardless
        of actual specifications. Reputable manufacturers publish their material test data and certification.
      </p>
      <h2>Denier Rating</h2>
      <p>
        Denier (D) measures the linear mass density of the polyester fabric core. Higher denier = thicker, stronger
        threads. Standard SUP PVC uses 1000D to 1600D fabric. 1000D is adequate for recreational boards under 220 lb
        capacity. 1600D is preferred for high-pressure touring boards, whitewater boards, and boards rated for 300+ lb
        capacity. Thread tensile strength at 1600D is approximately 40% higher than 1000D. Afarer uses 1600D fabric for
        all SUP models rated above 220 lb capacity.
      </p>
      <h2>PVC Coating Weight</h2>
      <p>
        PVC coating is applied to both sides of the polyester fabric. Total coating weight is measured in g/m² (grams
        per square meter). Standard coating: 600-800 g/m² total (300-400 g/m² per side). Heavy-duty coating: 900-1100
        g/m² total. Heavier coating improves abrasion resistance and air retention but adds weight and stiffness to the
        fabric. The coating must be evenly applied — uneven coating creates weak spots that may fail under pressure.
      </p>
      <h2>Total Fabric Thickness</h2>
      <p>
        Measured in millimeters, total thickness includes the fabric core plus both PVC coating layers. Entry-level:
        0.6-0.8mm. Standard quality: 0.9-1.0mm. Premium: 1.1-1.2mm. Whitewater/heavy duty: 1.2-1.5mm. Thicker PVC is
        more puncture-resistant and holds pressure longer but is heavier and stiffer to fold and pack. The industry
        trend is toward thinner, higher-strength PVC using advanced coating formulations rather than simply adding
        material thickness.
      </p>
      <h2>Key Quality Indicators</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cold crack resistance:</strong> Tested at -20°C to -30°C — quality PVC does not crack when flexed at low
          temperatures.
        </li><li>
          <strong>Hydrolysis resistance:</strong> Resistance to chemical breakdown in warm, humid conditions — critical for
          tropical use.
        </li><li>
          <strong>Flex crack resistance:</strong> Measured by repeated folding (DeMattia flex test) — premium PVC survives
          100,000+ flex cycles without cracking.
        </li><li>
          <strong>Color fastness:</strong> Rated on the blue wool scale (1-8) — minimum 6 for SUP applications.
        </li><li>
          <strong>Flame retardancy:</strong> Some applications require self-extinguishing PVC per UL 94 or equivalent standards.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
